# macOS
.DS_Store
.env

# Credentials / certificates (defensive — nothing here should ever exist tracked)
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
id_rsa*

# Local Project-Specific Records (Ignored by Global Submodule)
#
# This block used to also exclude the pipeline's own records — docs/sprints/,
# docs/active_state.json, implementation_plan*, task_scope.md — to keep a host's
# work out of the shared nucleus when .agents is installed as a submodule. That
# intent is right and RA-15 states it. The instrument was wrong, and inverted:
#
#   `close_workflow.md` submodule_purity guards the submodule with
#   `git -C .agents status --porcelain` is clean — and that command DOES NOT
#   LIST IGNORED FILES. Verified: a file created under docs/sprints/ left
#   `git status --porcelain` completely empty. The ignore did not protect the
#   nucleus from host contamination; it hid the contamination from the one
#   check built to catch it.
#
# The cost was equally concrete. `rules/documentation_standard.md:94` mandates a
# **git-tracked** graph_stats.json inside the canonical sprint directory — a
# tracked file in an ignored path, impossible to satisfy, so the freshness
# gate's delta series could never accumulate here. And with the anchor AND its
# mirror both excluded, the nucleus's entire state lived on one machine with no
# recoverable copy: the exact single point of failure the Phase 019 drift
# recovery was about.
#
# The rule is now stated where it can be enforced instead of assumed: a host
# session MUST NOT write inside .agents (agents.md §3 strict_rule), and
# submodule_purity refuses the close when it does — now that it can see.
#
# The line drawn is CONTENT vs SESSION STATE, not "sprint records yes/no":
#
#   Versioned — docs/sprints/[ID]/ : plan, task_scope, assignments, sprint log,
#     phase register, graph_stats. These describe work that happened. Excluding
#     them is what killed the four mechanisms above.
#
#   Not versioned — docs/active_state.json : it carries two lifetimes in one
#     file, durable (last_close_commit, current_sprint, acknowledged_gaps) and
#     volatile (session_id, start_time, status). Tracking it would ship the
#     nucleus's live session into every host checkout, and a host session that
#     ran any script with cwd=.agents would read and write the nucleus's own
#     anchor. Its durable half is recoverable from the tracked sprint record,
#     the ledger and the tags — which is exactly what Sprint 024 built. Splitting
#     the file by lifetime is a separate concern and is not invented here.
task/task.md
task/
task/sprints/
task/roadmaps/
docs/active_state.json
docs/active_task.md
.agent_state/
.agent_state/session_metadata.json

# IDE and OS Temp files
*~
*.sw[a-z]
*.tmp
.idea/
.vscode/

# (task_scope.md was excluded here as a "session artifact". It is not one: it is
# what jurisdictional_lock and no_interference are applied by reading, what
# loop_guard.py measures progress from, and what close_workflow.md Phase 2.6
# demands as phase evidence. Excluding it made all three unenforceable in the
# nucleus. It now lives inside the sprint directory and is versioned.)

# Machine-local install markers and environments
installed.lock
.bridge_claude.lock
.bridge_cursor.lock
.deploy_unlock
venv_skillopt/
venv/

# Generated knowledge graph output
graphify-out/

# Dependencies (install at runtime, never commit)
node_modules/

# Project Temp directory
tmp/

# Python Cache files
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Ephemeral memory (agents.md §4) — purged at close, never versioned
memory/

# Nucleus self-bridge (regenerable via scripts/install_claude.sh)
.claude/

# Cursor bridge output (regenerable via scripts/install.py --target cursor)
.cursor/commands/
.cursor/rules/
.cursor/mcp.json
